<section>
    <h2>Q Units & Physical Size</h2>

    <p>The Q unit provides a bridge between screen and print through its physical definition:</p>

    <div class="api-signature">1Q = 0.25mm (physical) = 0.0625rem (CSS) ≈ 1px (at 16px base)</div>

    <h3>Screen vs Print</h3>

    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Q Value</th>
                <th>Physical Size</th>
                <th>CSS Size</th>
                <th>Physical Pixels (1×)</th>
                <th>Physical Pixels (2×)</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>1Q</td>
                <td>0.25mm</td>
                <td>0.0625rem</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>16Q</td>
                <td>4mm</td>
                <td>1rem</td>
                <td>16</td>
                <td>32</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>160Q (Q1)</td>
                <td>40mm</td>
                <td>10rem</td>
                <td>160</td>
                <td>320</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>320Q (Q2)</td>
                <td>80mm</td>
                <td>20rem</td>
                <td>320</td>
                <td>640</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>This unified system means you can design for screen and print using the same Q values. When printing, Q units map
        directly to physical millimeters. On screen, they adapt to the display's density while maintaining proportional
        relationships.</p>
</section>
